WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.400
Jason "Wee Man" Acuña might be the most&nbsp;
recognizable member of the "Jackass" cast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.400
but this skateboarder's got some tricks you&nbsp;
haven't seen – and some tacos you have to try.

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:13.680
Jason Acuña's father George served in the United&nbsp;
States Army and was stationed in Germany when he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:18.000
met and married Acuña's mother. The couple was&nbsp;
transferred to a base in Pisa, Italy, while his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:22.240
mother was pregnant. Though English is his first&nbsp;
language, Acuña's mother taught him German as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.240 --> 00:00:27.360
boy, and he remains fluent in the language. He&nbsp;
told Steve-O on the Steve-O Wild Ride Podcast,

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:30.160
"You know when you're a kid, and&nbsp;
you’re asking your mom, 'What's this?'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.640
My mom would tell me in German, and I'd&nbsp;
be like, 'No, not that funny language!'"

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.720
Eventually, Acuña would settle in southern&nbsp;
California, where his father's family is from,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.480
and as a preteen, he became heavily involved&nbsp;
in the area's skateboarding culture.

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.680
When he was born, the delivering doctor noticed&nbsp;
that Jason Acuña's head was large compared to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.720
rest of his body. Concerned that the baby might be&nbsp;
hydrocephalic, the doctor ran tests and determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:57.680
that Acuña instead had a genetic condition&nbsp;
known as achondroplasia, which causes dwarfism.

00:00:57.680 --> 00:01:01.120
As a boy, Acuña's mother took him to&nbsp;
an annual conference sponsored by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.640
support group Little People of America,&nbsp;
thinking that he might enjoy meeting other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:09.840
children with dwarfism. Acuña felt differently,&nbsp;
however, telling The New York Times in 2022,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:13.280
"I came back and I go, 'Mom, I don't&nbsp;
want to go to these things anymore.'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.600
I'm like, 'That's not what my life is about."

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.400
He compared the impulse to group&nbsp;
people with dwarfism together to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:21.200
the idea that a bald man should&nbsp;
be friends with other bald men.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:24.880
"He's a dude, he's a jogger, he wants to&nbsp;
hang out with other joggers. Skateboarder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:25.840
hang out with skateboarders."

00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:32.240
As a young skateboarder in Torrance, California,&nbsp;
in the late 1980s and early 1990s, Jason Acuña&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.400
began attracting attention. In his interview with&nbsp;
The New York Times, he said he was already being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.200
sponsored by local skate shops at age 14. At the&nbsp;
time, the local Torrance skateboard company was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:44.880
World Industries, and Acuña and his friends&nbsp;
would try to score free merchandise at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:50.400
company warehouse. Opened in 1987 by professional&nbsp;
skateboarders Steve Rocco and Rodney Mullen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:54.880
World Industries billed itself as the first&nbsp;
skateboard company operated by and for skaters.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:58.400
The story goes that Steve Rocco's&nbsp;
brother Sal gave Acuña his nickname,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:01.760
shouting "Hey everyone! Wee Man is&nbsp;
here!" whenever Acuña would come around.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:05.280
A few years later, World Industries'&nbsp;
magazine Big Brother published a photo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.240
spread featuring Acuña and printed the nickname.&nbsp;
After that, Wee Man was his official stage name.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:14.000
Much of the groundwork for what&nbsp;
would one day become "Jackass"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.600
was laid at Big Brother magazine, the&nbsp;
publishing arm of World Industries.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:22.640
Started by Steve Rocco in 1992, the magazine&nbsp;
embraced its parent company's "for us, by us"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:26.880
ethos and ran skateboarding interviews and&nbsp;
photo spreads alongside features and humor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:31.360
pieces that were unpolished, irreverent,&nbsp;
and raunchy. In other words, it was…

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:33.520
"'Jackass' before 'Jackass.'"

00:02:33.520 --> 00:02:37.200
Acuña got a job in the office by virtue&nbsp;
of being local and hanging around so much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:41.200
that editor and future "Jackass" director&nbsp;
Jeff Tremaine finally just put him to work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:43.760
When Big Brother released its&nbsp;
first video — a skateboarding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.760 --> 00:02:47.120
compilation — Acuña appeared on&nbsp;
the box art covered in blue paint.

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.320
There is an egalitarian streak that runs&nbsp;
through the two-plus decade run of "Jackass."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:56.240
Everyone suffers on camera at some point,&nbsp;
and everyone is the butt of the joke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.880
Over the years, many of Acuña's&nbsp;
stunts and pranks have been no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:01.920
different from the ones performed by&nbsp;
Knoxville or the rest of the cast.

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:05.440
But many of his best and most memorable&nbsp;
stunts came from the unique opportunities&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.440 --> 00:03:09.760
afforded by his height. Acuña was often&nbsp;
paired with cast member Preston Lacy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.520
as their two polar-opposite body types&nbsp;
complement one another to comedic effect.

00:03:14.480 --> 00:03:18.000
Because of his stature, Jason Acuña&nbsp;
is the most recognized "Jackass"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:22.160
cast member — even more so than Knoxville.&nbsp;
His fame is inescapable, and he estimates that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:26.720
he's been recognized on the street nearly every&nbsp;
day since the show premiered in the year 2000.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.280
Over the years, Acuña has avoided the tabloid&nbsp;
attention that some of his castmates have faced.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:35.440
In fact, he has discovered that the name "Wee&nbsp;
Man" serves a useful role in delineating those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:38.960
people who want to be his friend from&nbsp;
those who simply want proximity to fame.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.960 --> 00:03:42.720
Speaking to Jenkem Magazine about&nbsp;
romantic relationships, Acuña said,

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:46.720
"No chick has ever called me Wee Man that&nbsp;
I've dated. I've also never dated a chick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.320
that I noticed would call me Wee Man because&nbsp;
it didn't feel like she was trying to date me."

00:03:50.960 --> 00:03:55.520
So if he's not wasting it on the spoils of&nbsp;
fame, what does Jason Acuña do with his money?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:59.120
In 2008, he became an investor in the&nbsp;
California-based Mexican restaurant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.120 --> 00:04:04.080
chain Chronic Tacos, opening a franchise in&nbsp;
Redondo Beach called "Wee Man's Chronic Tacos."

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.320
The chain was a good fit for him, not just because&nbsp;
of the rock and roll street art aesthetic of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:12.560
locations, but also as a connection to his&nbsp;
father's family, which is of Mexican descent.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:18.560
In 2012, Jason Acuña took a rare leading man&nbsp;
role in the family Christmas comedy "Elf-Man."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:22.560
When a little girl makes a wish on an old elf&nbsp;
doll to bring her father home on Christmas Eve, a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:27.200
magical elf, played by Acuña, appears to make the&nbsp;
wish come true by morning, when Santa will bring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:31.040
him back to the North Pole. Little do they know&nbsp;
that the little girl's father has been kidnapped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:35.440
by a group of thieves seeking his fantastic new&nbsp;
invention. When the thieves attack the house, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:40.240
elf must defend the family by believing in himself&nbsp;
and becoming the Christmas superhero Elf-Man.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:45.200
The film was released direct to video and was not&nbsp;
terribly well-received. The most positive review&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.200 --> 00:04:50.000
might be this YouTube video featuring the children&nbsp;
of Acuña's "Jackass" castmate Dave England.

00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.240
"I thought it was really funny that&nbsp;
the robbers were not very smart.

